1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,552
Narrator: Scenario 62. Monitoring, performance, and reliability.

2
00:00:07,152 --> 00:00:17,928
B: One database failure generated fifty pages overnight. I spent several minutes opening duplicate incidents before I could identify the shared dependency.

3
00:00:18,528 --> 00:00:29,472
A: Let us separate repeated notifications from distinct symptoms. Which alerts shared the same database cluster, and which belonged to unrelated services?

4
00:00:30,072 --> 00:00:40,152
B: Forty-two pages came from services using the east cluster. Eight came from a separate batch system and should remain independent notifications.

5
00:00:40,752 --> 00:00:51,432
C: We can group the east-cluster alerts into one incident. Should the database alert also suppress every application alert associated with that cluster?

6
00:00:52,032 --> 00:01:02,112
A: Only where we have verified the dependency. An application may have another failure at the same time, so blanket suppression could hide impact.

7
00:01:02,712 --> 00:01:14,208
C: Then we can suppress duplicate pages while keeping downstream events visible on the incident timeline. Critical user journey failures can retain separate routing.

8
00:01:14,808 --> 00:01:24,360
B: What happens if the database alert clears first but checkout is still failing? I need the application problem to become actionable again.

9
00:01:24,960 --> 00:01:36,768
A: We should test that transition explicitly. Once suppression ends, unresolved application alerts must notify their owners according to the configured notification policy.

10
00:01:37,368 --> 00:01:49,512
C: I will create tests for dependency failure, recovery, missing labels, and simultaneous independent incidents. Incorrect labels will fall back to normal routing.

11
00:01:50,112 --> 00:02:00,480
B: Please also check the destination team. Last night the database page reached the application rotation, which added another ten minutes of delay.

12
00:02:01,080 --> 00:02:12,192
A: I will verify ownership with both teams today. We can review test results tomorrow at 16:00 UTC before enabling any new suppression rules.

13
00:02:12,792 --> 00:02:24,096
C: Agreed. After approval, we will monitor duplicate page counts and missed escalations for seven days, with the old routing available for rollback.
